## Top 3 Prioritized Fixes (in order of impact)

| # | Fix | Why it matters | How to implement (quick checklist) |
|---|-----|----------------|--------------------------------------|
| **1** | **Boost ATS compatibility** | ATS systems score resumes on keyword hits and parsable lists. A missed keyword or malformed “Skills” block can drop you out of the pile before a recruiter even sees it. | • Insert a **bulleted “Technical Skills”** section (e.g., “SQL, Python, R, Tableau, Power BI, ETL, Data Modeling, Statistical Analysis, Machine Learning”). <br>• Add the missing **keywords** naturally in the experience bullet points (e.g., “Designed ETL pipelines in SQL Server” or “Performed statistical modeling for forecasting”). <br>• Standardize date format to **MM/YYYY–MM/YYYY** (or just years) and ensure all dates are *past* dates. |
| **2** | **Resolve grammar & formatting inconsistencies** | Clean, consistent formatting conveys professionalism and prevents ATS mis‑reading of sections. | • End **all bullet points with periods** (or none—pick one style and apply consistently). <br>• Align spacing after colons (e.g., “Experience:”). <br>• Rewrite the **Skills** line as a parallel list, not a sentence (“SQL, Python, Tableau, Excel, Stakeholder Communication”). <br>• Complete the **Education** entry: `B.S. Statistics, University of XYZ, City, State – May 2022`. |
| **3** | **Add concrete technical depth** | Recruiters and hiring managers want to gauge the *scale* and *complexity* of your work. Detailing volume, tools, and advanced methods differentiates you from other candidates with similar titles. | • For each role, specify **data volume** (e.g., “processed >10 M rows daily from Oracle and Snowflake”). <br>• Mention any **advanced analytics** you performed (e.g., “built predictive churn model using logistic regression”, “implemented time‑series forecasting with ARIMA”). <br>• Highlight exposure to **additional tools/platforms** (e.g., “utilized Power BI for executive reporting”, “performed R‑based statistical tests”). |

